# tools/r/rish_report_bound_witness.rish -- a guard's success report has a ceiling; its refusal does not.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/rish_report_bound_witness.rish
#
# On `20260907` `reds_spine_derive_witness.rish` read this tree, PASSED both of its gates --
# `verdict=ok`, `rebindings=0` -- and then ended the run at its own reporting line. Rishi composes
# an interpolated string literal through `StrBuf`, a bounded 4,096-byte buffer in
# rishi/src/main.rye, and refuses `StringTooLong` past it. The reading had grown to 4,250 bytes as
# the ledger grew, so the seven-word label wrapped around it is what refused. Eight ships paid a
# red every lap for a green reading, and the message named the guard's reporting line rather than
# anything about the tree.
#
# THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING. An `assert ... else` message that overflows falls back to its raw
# literal -- `interpolate(...) catch return text` -- so a REFUSAL degrades rather than dies. A
# `say` has no fallback. Rishi already protects the path that fires when something is wrong; the
# unprotected path is the one that fires when everything is right, and a success report is exactly
# what grows without bound as the tree it reads grows. The control proves all three claims against
# the real interpreter on metal rather than reading them out of main.rye.
#
# The repair is two lines and reads better: the label on its own `say`, the reading on a bare one.
# This guard obeys its own law -- every reading below is printed bare.

say "rish-report-bound: Language EN -- a guard that reds on a green reading is worse than no guard."
say "rish-report-bound: Style Gauge, Meter -- the ceiling is read from Rishi's own source, never spelled twice."
say "rish-report-bound: Lens TAME -- bound everything, and prove the bound from both sides on metal."

say "rish-report-bound: the control -- fifteen behaviors, three of them against the real interpreter..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rish_report_bound_control.sh"]
assert ctl.ok else "rish-report-bound: the control refused --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"
assert ctl.out contains "pass=15 fail=0" else "rish-report-bound: the control did not prove fifteen behaviors --\n${ctl.out}"

say "rish-report-bound: and the living tree, read on metal..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rish_report_bound_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "rish-report-bound: the scan would not run -- read its own lines"
say scan.out

# invariant: the ratchet is a CEILING, compared with -le inside the scan, so a repair can never
# red. A floor spelled as an equality refused the rise it existed to reward seven times in one day
# (REDS %584). The verdict is asserted rather than a number copied here, so a fall costs one
# deliberate hand in one file rather than two that may disagree (REDS %341's class).
assert scan.out contains "verdict=under_ceiling" else "rish-report-bound: a guard composes a report that may outgrow the buffer -- run with --sites, then put the label on its own say and the reading on a bare one"

# invariant: the buffer is READ from rishi/src/main.rye rather than spelled in the scan. Were that
# read to fail, the scan would print `strbuf_bytes=unknown` and go on counting against a ceiling
# nobody could interpret -- so the reading is asserted here, where it is cheap to see.
assert scan.out contains "strbuf_bytes=4096" else "rish-report-bound: the StrBuf bound could not be read from rishi/src/main.rye, so the reading names no ceiling"

say "GREEN: rish-report-bound -- Rishi refuses an interpolated say past 4,096 bytes and lets a bare one through, an oversized assert message falls back to its literal instead of ending the run, and every tracked guard that composes a verdict-bearing report into a sentence stands under a ceiling that only falls."
